      </div>

      <h2>The 7 Most Common WhatsApp Link Errors (And Instant Fixes)</h2>

      <h3>Error 1: "The phone number shared via url is invalid"</h3>
      <p>This is by far the #1 error encountered by businesses worldwide. When clicked, WhatsApp loads a white error screen stating:</p>
      <pre><code>"The phone number shared via url is invalid."</code></pre>
      <p><strong>The Cause:</strong> You included a plus sign (<code>+</code>), spaces, brackets, or leading zeros before your country code.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>❌ <code>https://wa.me/+1(415)555-2671</code> (Contains +, brackets, and dashes)</li>
        <li>❌ <code>https://wa.me/00447911123456</code> (Contains leading international zeros 00)</li>
        <li>❌ <code>https://wa.me/09876543210</code> (Contains local trunk prefix 0)</li>
        <li>✅ <code>https://wa.me/14155552671</code> (Pure numeric string)</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Strip every character except numbers. Combine the country code directly with your national number without spaces.</p>

      <h3>Error 2: Missing Country Code</h3>
      <p>Many users type their local 10-digit mobile number and wonder why the link fails to connect:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>❌ <code>https://wa.me/9876543210</code> (WhatsApp does not know which country this number belongs to)</li>
        <li>✅ <code>https://wa.me/919876543210</code> (Prefix 91 explicitly declares India)</li>
        <li>✅ <code>https://wa.me/447911123456</code> (Prefix 44 explicitly declares United Kingdom)</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Always verify your international dialing code before creating the link.</p>

      <h3>Error 3: Broken Pre-Filled Message Due to URL Encoding Failures</h3>
      <p>If you want a pre-filled text to open in the chat, you append <code>?text=Your%20Message</code>. However, typing raw spaces or symbols directly breaks the URL in browsers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>❌ <code>https://wa.me/919876543210?text=Hi, I want info!</code> (Browsers cut off the link at the first space)</li>
        <li>✅ <code>https://wa.me/919876543210?text=Hi%2C%20I%20want%20info%21</code> (Safely URL-encoded)</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Every space must be written as <code>%20</code>, and special punctuation must be percent-encoded. Instead of doing this by hand, use <a href="/">LinkWA</a>, which auto-encodes emojis, commas, and line breaks automatically.</p>

      <h3>Error 4: Instagram or TikTok In-App Browser Blocking the Redirect</h3>
      <p>When users tap a WhatsApp link inside Instagram or TikTok, the platform opens its internal in-app web browser rather than Safari or Chrome. In-app browsers frequently fail to trigger native deep-link schemes (like <code>whatsapp://</code>).</p>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Always use standard <code>https://wa.me/...</code> URLs or short branded links (like <code>linkwa.in/yourname</code>). Standard HTTPS protocols smoothly trigger the deep-link prompt across iOS and Android without getting trapped in the in-app browser sandbox.</p>

      <h3>Error 5: Using Dead or Expired Competitor Short Links (wa.link, bit.ly)</h3>
      <p>Competitor link services like <code>wa.link</code> often lock custom branded links behind annual subscriptions ($6/year). If payment lapses or the third-party domain encounters downtime, your marketing link goes dead overnight. Generic bit.ly links also trigger aggressive spam warnings on Meta platforms.</p>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Switch to <a href="/">LinkWA</a>. Our links are 100% free forever, backed by Cloudflare's ultra-fast global edge network with 99.99% uptime.</p>

      <h3>Error 6: Desktop Browser Opening WhatsApp Web Instead of App (or vice versa)</h3>
      <p>When someone taps a link on a desktop computer, they might see a screen asking them to <em>"Download WhatsApp"</em> even if they already have WhatsApp Web open in another tab.</p>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Modern <code>wa.me</code> links display an official intermediate landing screen with two clear options: <strong>"Continue to Chat"</strong> and <strong>"use WhatsApp Web"</strong>. If a user gets stuck, advise them to click <em>"use WhatsApp Web"</em> to open the chat instantly in their active browser session.</p>

      <h3>Error 7: The Phone Number is Not Registered on WhatsApp</h3>
      <p>Even if the syntax is 100% correct, if the target phone number does not currently have an active WhatsApp or WhatsApp Business account registered, WhatsApp displays a generic error: <em>"Phone number is not on WhatsApp."</em></p>
      <p><strong>The Fix:</strong> Open WhatsApp on your own phone and send a manual test message to that exact number. Verify that the checkmarks turn delivered.</p>

